  "image_name": "4e7d3bd7df92e4c8b15ea8e7feb7b1a0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes pathways for managing saline brine after treatment. Brine first enters a pretreatment step, illustrated with an example such as softening, and then moves to a concentration stage based on reverse osmosis, possibly followed by other brine-concentration methods. From there, the process splits into two broad outcomes.\n\nThe upper route leads toward zero liquid discharge. In this path, the concentrated brine undergoes crystallization, either through an engineered process or a natural one. The engineered option is brine crystallization equipment that is labeled as achieving about 100% water recovery. The natural option is an evaporation pond, where water is lost as vapor. Both approaches ultimately produce salt crystals as the remaining solid product.\n\nThe lower route represents minimal liquid discharge rather than full recovery. Instead of crystallization, the remaining brine is sent out of the facility for disposal, either by deep-well injection underground or by surface discharge to a receiving water body.\n\nOverall, the graphic contrasts closed-loop, salt-producing management of brine with more conventional off-site disposal routes, showing how increasing concentration can lead either to near-complete water recovery or to liquid waste release.",
